Diagnosis:
The test `testBoundaryRangeTooLarge` creates a scenario where the lower and upper bounds passed to the CMAESOptimizer are extremely large, close to ±Double.MAX_VALUE. The test expects a `NumberIsTooLargeException` to be thrown.

The issue report indicates that when very wide bounds (large difference between upper and lower bounds) are used, the optimizer calls the fitness function with parameters set to NaN. The report suspects this is due to the encode/decode methods in the FitnessFunction class generating NaN because normalization involves dividing by the difference between upper and lower bounds.

Looking at the encode method from the FitnessFunction class inside CMAESOptimizer:
```
public double[] encode(final double[] x) {
    if (boundaries == null) {
        return x;
    }
    double[] res = new double[x.length];
    for (int i = 0; i < x.length; i++) {
        double diff = boundaries[1][i] - boundaries[0][i];
        res[i] = (x[i] - boundaries[0][i]) / diff;
    }
    return res;
}
```

The method normalizes the parameter values by subtracting the lower bound and dividing by the range (diff). If diff is very large (close to Double.MAX_VALUE), the computation `(x[i] - boundaries[0][i]) / diff` can result in an overflow or produce NaN because the difference might overflow or be too large for double precision.

Hence, the root cause is that no safeguard or check prevents encode from producing NaN when bounds are extremely large. This leads the optimizer to work with NaN values, ultimately calling the fitness function with NaN parameters.

This matches the failure in the test, which expects an exception due to the bounds being too large, but instead the system progresses to NaN values in the parameters.

